      "company": "Google / Netflix / Amazon",
      "prompt": "What happens under the hood when open() is invoked, and how can resource leakage lead to process failure?",
      "q": "What happens under the hood when open() is invoked, and how can resource leakage lead to process failure?",
      "solution": "<p><em>A: When <code>open()</code> is called, the CPython runtime issues an <code>open()</code> or <code>openat()</code> syscall to the OS kernel. The kernel allocates an entry in the system-wide open file table and returns a non-negative integer—the <strong>file descriptor (fd)</strong>—to the calling process table. The operating system caps the number of concurrent file descriptors per process (configured via <code>ulimit -n</code>, standard default 1024). If an application creates file handles without closing them (e.g. failing to use context managers), file descriptors remain bound. Once the cap is hit, every subsequent attempt to open files, bind network sockets, or accept TCP connections fails with <code>OSError: [Errno 24] Too many open files</code>. Using <code>with open(...) as f:</code> guarantees RAII cleanup by executing <code>close()</code> deterministically via <code>__exit__</code>.</em></p>",
      "a": "<p><em>A: When <code>open()</code> is called, the CPython runtime issues an <code>open()</code> or <code>openat()</code> syscall to the OS kernel. The kernel allocates an entry in the system-wide open file table and returns a non-negative integer—the <strong>file descriptor (fd)</strong>—to the calling process table. The operating system caps the number of concurrent file descriptors per process (configured via <code>ulimit -n</code>, standard default 1024). If an application creates file handles without closing them (e.g. failing to use context managers), file descriptors remain bound. Once the cap is hit, every subsequent attempt to open files, bind network sockets, or accept TCP connections fails with <code>OSError: [Errno 24] Too many open files</code>. Using <code>with open(...) as f:</code> guarantees RAII cleanup by executing <code>close()</code> deterministically via <code>__exit__</code>.</em></p>"
