      "prompt": "Why does writing CSV files without newline='' introduce blank rows on Windows, and what is Python's universal newline mechanism doing?",
      "q": "Why does writing CSV files without newline='' introduce blank rows on Windows, and what is Python's universal newline mechanism doing?",
      "solution": "<p><em>A: Python text mode features universal newline translation: by default, any <code>\\n</code> written to a text stream on Windows is automatically converted by the C runtime into the platform convention <code>\\r\\n</code> (CRLF). However, the <code>csv.writer</code> module explicitly formats rows according to RFC 4180, appending <code>\\r\\n</code> as its line terminator. Without <code>newline=''</code>, Python's text layer intercepts the <code>\\n</code> within <code>\\r\\n</code> and expands it to <code>\\r\\n</code>, producing <code>\\r\\r\\n</code> on disk. Microsoft Excel parses <code>\\r</code> as a carriage return and <code>\\n</code> as a newline, creating an empty blank row between every record. Setting <code>newline=''</code> disables Python's automatic translation layer and lets <code>csv.writer</code> control termination directly.</em></p>",
      "a": "<p><em>A: Python text mode features universal newline translation: by default, any <code>\\n</code> written to a text stream on Windows is automatically converted by the C runtime into the platform convention <code>\\r\\n</code> (CRLF). However, the <code>csv.writer</code> module explicitly formats rows according to RFC 4180, appending <code>\\r\\n</code> as its line terminator. Without <code>newline=''</code>, Python's text layer intercepts the <code>\\n</code> within <code>\\r\\n</code> and expands it to <code>\\r\\n</code>, producing <code>\\r\\r\\n</code> on disk. Microsoft Excel parses <code>\\r</code> as a carriage return and <code>\\n</code> as a newline, creating an empty blank row between every record. Setting <code>newline=''</code> disables Python's automatic translation layer and lets <code>csv.writer</code> control termination directly.</em></p>"
