      "q": "Why do production analytics pipelines universally prefer JSON Lines (.jsonl) over standard monolithic JSON files?",
      "solution": "<p><em>A: A monolithic JSON file is a single enclosed tree (e.g. <code>[{...}, {...}]</code>). This forces three architectural limitations: (1) <strong>Atomic Parsing:</strong> The parser must ingest the closing bracket before the document is valid; a single corrupted byte at byte 9,999,999 invalidates the entire file. (2) <strong>Memory Footprint:</strong> Parsing a 10GB JSON file requires constructing the entire object hierarchy in RAM, exceeding worker memory limits. (3) <strong>Append Impossibility:</strong> Adding a record requires reading the file, deleting the closing bracket, inserting the record, and re-writing. In contrast, <strong>JSON Lines (<code>.jsonl</code>)</strong> places one independent JSON document per line: records can be appended concurrently in $O(1)$ time, streaming workers process items with $O(1)$ RAM, and invalid lines can be quarantined to a dead-letter queue without losing the dataset.</em></p>",
