      "q": "How do you guarantee atomic file updates in Python so that concurrent readers never observe corrupted, half-written data if a server loses power?",
      "solution": "<p><em>A: Directly opening a target file with <code>open('state.json', 'w')</code> immediately truncates the file to 0 bytes; if the process crashes mid-write or the machine reboots, the target file is permanently corrupted. The industry production recipe is <strong>Atomic Rename via Temporary File</strong>: (1) Write data to a temporary file located on the <em>same filesystem/mount</em> using <code>tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(dir=target.parent, delete=False)</code>. (2) Call <code>f.flush()</code> and <code>os.fsync(f.fileno())</code> to force all dirty OS page cache buffers to physical disk platters. (3) Execute <code>os.replace(temp_path, target_path)</code>. On POSIX and modern Windows NTFS, <code>os.replace</code> is an atomic directory metadata swap: readers either see the old complete version or the new complete version—never a partial state.</em></p>",
      "a": "<p><em>A: Directly opening a target file with <code>open('state.json', 'w')</code> immediately truncates the file to 0 bytes; if the process crashes mid-write or the machine reboots, the target file is permanently corrupted. The industry production recipe is <strong>Atomic Rename via Temporary File</strong>: (1) Write data to a temporary file located on the <em>same filesystem/mount</em> using <code>tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(dir=target.parent, delete=False)</code>. (2) Call <code>f.flush()</code> and <code>os.fsync(f.fileno())</code> to force all dirty OS page cache buffers to physical disk platters. (3) Execute <code>os.replace(temp_path, target_path)</code>. On POSIX and modern Windows NTFS, <code>os.replace</code> is an atomic directory metadata swap: readers either see the old complete version or the new complete version—never a partial state.</em></p>"
